course: dictionary
lesson: 14335
title: Formal words in reports
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words belong to formal writing, like reports, letters and exam passages. Writers use them to sound precise. «A new bridge is obviating the need for the long ferry trip.» The clue is in the rest of the sentence: a new bridge means you no longer have to take the ferry, so obviating must mean removing a need.
explain: Look for the job the word does in the sentence. Obviating is followed by "the need for", which tells you it is about something no longer being necessary. Try an easier phrase in its place, such as "so we don't need", and check the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Formal words often replace longer, clumsier phrases. «Carrying a spare battery obviates the need to find a plug» means exactly the same as «Carrying a spare battery means you don't need to find a plug.» Obviating is shorter and sounds more formal, which is why it is used in reports and exams.
fact: Obviating is a formal word that means removing a need or problem, so that something is no longer necessary.
fact: «Obviating the need for» means "so there is no need for".
fact: «A ramp obviates the need for steps» means a ramp means you do not need steps.
fact: Obviate is the root word, and obviates, obviated and obviating are its other forms.
fact: Everyday words for obviating are "avoiding" or "so we don't need", but a formal writer would choose obviating.
exercise: «Carrying a spare battery is obviating the need to find a plug.» What does obviating mean here? | Making a need bigger | Breaking a plug | Searching for something | *Removing the need for something
exercise: Dad says, «Taking the train is obviating the need for a car.» Which everyday phrase could replace obviating? | *So we don't need | So we need more | So we are late | So we must wait
exercise: «Using a calculator obviates long sums on paper.» Find and copy the word that tells you the calculator takes away the need for long sums. | *obviates | paper | using | calculator
test: «Solar panels obviate the need for a power cable to the shed.» What does obviate mean? | Painting the shed | Running out of power | *Removing the need | Creating a problem
test: Which sentence uses obviating correctly? | "The obviating road is very long." | "I obviate my dinner every night." | *"A ramp obviates the need for steps." | "Obviating is a fast running shoe."
exam: «Her sat-nav is obviating the need for a paper map.» Which phrase means the same as obviating here? | so she must buy a paper map | so she is lost on the road | *so she does not need a paper map | so she will draw her own map
